3	// The subject of the printer panel's conversation: what the model is told and
4	// may call there, and the cards the page draws in the thread.
5	//
6	// The conversation is the whole panel. The model does the understanding --
7	// which printer someone has, what changed on one they own, how to reach it --
8	// and acts through the session's tools, which check requests against the
9	// printer data and carry them out. printer_connect collects its credential in
10	// a local form so it never passes through the model. The other things to tap
11	// are Undo on an added printer's receipt and the printer form, where the
12	// person picks a printer from the lists themselves; the model hears about
13	// either in a note.
14	//
15	// A session is opened for one printer question -- add a printer, change this
16	// one, connect this one -- and is discarded when the panel closes. Nothing
17	// here is written to the project: AgentHost keeps the thread in an in-memory
18	// document, and printers are saved through IPrinterBackend, which owns the
19	// only route to Orca.
20	//
21	// GUI-free and Orca-free: the panel supplies both interfaces, and the tests
22	// supply fakes.
